From 4249721acff1ef04b78ad58a57493cf5a69d327a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Upstream Contributor <contributor@example.invalid>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 12:37:35 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] feat(eval): add MultiJail and Aya Red-teaming benchmarks

Every built-in safety benchmark is English, so there is no way to measure
whether a repair holds in other languages.

Add multijail (315 harmful prompts in each of 10 languages, translated) and
aya_redteaming (7,419 prompts written by native speakers of 8 languages) to
the dataset table, the evaluate() registry and load_prompts(). Each row has
a language column, and languages= picks a subset.

MultiJail has a column per language, so its rows are unpivoted into one row
per prompt and language. Aya Red-teaming has one split per language and no
train split, so its table entry names no split and the loader asks for each
one. A split set in the table loads only that split, for a local copy.

This adds data only. The refusal string lists are English and the default
judge has not been validated on these languages, and a refusal a judge
can't read counts as an attack success. The docs and the registry
descriptions say so.
